loit               1\nName: MitreTechniques, dtype: int64"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":4},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## Pairs worth real scrutiny\n\n### `EntityType` ↔ `EvidenceRole`\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Cramér's V | 0.88 |\n| Row Overlap | 9,494,278 (~99.9% of dataset) |\n\nThis is the highest-coverage high-correlation pair — backed by essentially the whole dataset, not a small-sample artifact.\n\nThe aggregate Cramér's V (0.88) is inflated by several rare entity types where EvidenceRole is perfectly one-sided (e.g., Ip and CloudLogonRequest are always \"Related\"; AzureResource is always \"Impacted\"). But for the highest-volume entity types — File (~54% Impacted / 46% Related) and MailMessage (~58% / 42%) — the split is close to even, meaning EntityType does not determine EvidenceRole for a large share of the dataset. EvidenceRole carries real, independent signal for these common categories, so it should be kept alongside EntityType.\n\n### `Category` ↔ `MitreTechniques`\n\n| Metric | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Cramér's V | 0.86 |\n| Row Overlap | 4,034,910 (~42.4% of dataset) |\n\nAlso backed by a large, meaningful chunk of the data — worth checking whether `MitreTechniques` adds information beyond what `Category` already captures, or whether it's largely redundant for the rows where both are present.\n\nChecking coverage showed MitreTechniques is filled in for most rows in some categories (CredentialAccess 79%, Discovery 89%, InitialAccess 74%) but almost never for others (Exfiltration, Impact, SuspiciousActivity all around 2%), which explains part of the high aggregate correlation. But checking diversity within the rows where it is present showed it's far from redundant — Discovery alone spans 231 distinct techniques, with Execution (144), CredentialAccess (137), LateralMovement (124), and Persistence (120) all showing similarly rich detail. So the 0.86 correlation reflects a genuine clustering effect (certain categories tend toward certain techniques) rather than one column just restating the other — both should be kept, with a plan to handle the uneven missingness (e.g., an explicit \"not recorded\" category) when encoding MitreTechniques.\n\n## Decision so far\n\n**No columns have been removed yet.** Every other high-correlation pair turned out to be explained by sparsity (rarely co-occurring), not true redundancy — so there's no basis to drop them yet.","metadata":{}}]}